specs/hal-engine-commit-open-entries/spec.md:8:A streaming entry is born with `isStreaming: true`, and only a commit flips it to `false` and sends its `entry_commit` frame. The commit used to run only on a `stop` chunk, so a provider that threw mid-stream, or a round that ended on a tool call with no `stop`, left the entry streaming in session state forever. That is invisible today only because a session is deleted when its socket closes; once stored entries are replayed on resume, a resumed client would render the never-committed entry as streaming for the rest of the conversation.
specs/hal-engine-commit-open-entries/spec.md:21:- Discarding it server-side would make the stored conversation disagree with what the customer already saw delta by delta, and a resumed transcript would be shorter than the live one was, so the content is committed exactly as streamed ([validated by: commits a partial answer as streamed, before the error, when the provider throws](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L414)).
specs/hal-engine-awaitable-session-store/spec.md:16:- The socket is paused before the awaited `create` and resumed only once the `message` listener is attached, so a client that sends its first frame in its `open` handler — before `connected` has arrived — is answered rather than ignored; the `connected` frame still precedes every entry frame ([validated by: delivers a frame sent before the connected frame arrives](../../src/transport/awaitedSessionCreate.test.ts#L33)).
specs/hal-engine-awaitable-session-store/spec.md:26:- **NIS-2 Article 21.** A `create` that rejects closes that one socket with an `error` frame carrying `SERVER_ERROR` and produces no `unhandledRejection`, since an async connection listener that rejects would otherwise end the process and take every other conversation on the server with it; the socket is resumed before it is closed, because `ws.close()` on a paused socket waits for a close frame it can never read and gives up only after the library's own thirty-second timeout ([validated by: answers a rejecting create with SERVER_ERROR rather than an unhandled rejection](../../src/transport/awaitedSessionCreate.test.ts#L56)).
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:45:- The helper decides "absent" structurally rather than with `instanceof Error`, because a module loader running in another realm throws an `Error` this one disowns — measured under jest, where `instanceof Error` is `false` for exactly that failure.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:150:`npm` is installed at `^11.5.1` rather than `latest` in the publish job. Trusted publishing needs 11.5.1 or newer, and that job is the one holding `id-token: write` - `latest` lets a major nobody here has run decide how the publish behaves.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:193:- `workflow_dispatch` exists for one case: re-running a publish that failed after the release already exists. The earlier objection to it - that a publish startable by hand takes whatever branch somebody picks in a dropdown - is answered by the guards rather than by removing the trigger. The version guard reads the ref name and refuses anything that is not a `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` tag, and the ancestry check refuses a commit that is not on `main`, so a branch chosen there fails closed. The one exception is the single-use bootstrap under § First publish, which is a separate workflow file, runs once, and is deleted in the same change that registers the trusted publisher.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:194:- Prereleases are refused rather than routed to a dist-tag, twice over: the job condition refuses a release ticked as a pre-release, and the version guard refuses the tag shape. Nothing here passes `--tag`, so a `v0.3.0-rc.1` would publish as `latest` and every plain `npm install` would resolve to it. Adding a dist-tag is a decision for whoever first needs one.
specs/hal-engine-npm-release/spec.md:198:- Staging needs npm >= 11.15.0, above the 11.5.1 trusted publishing needs, so the job installs `npm@^11.15.0` rather than `npm@^11.5.1`. It is pinned to the 11.x line rather than `latest` because this is the one job holding `id-token: write`.
